﻿using Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting;
using SampleWeb;
using SampleWeb.Controllers;
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using SampleWeb.Models;

namespace SampleWeb.Test
{
    [TestClass]
    public class HomeControllerTest
    {
        [TestMethod]
        public void IndexViewResultNotNull()
        {
            // Arrange
            var controller = new HomeController(null);

            //--------------------            int previous = arrList.Count;


            // Assuming that new value of count property is greater than or equal to previous count and can not be bigger than MAX_VALUE
            foreach (var operation in PexHelper.Iterate(a => (a >= previous) && (a < int.MaxValue)))
            {
                // This is Pex generated code
                arrList.Count = operation;


                int after = arrList.Count;


                if (!(after >= previous && after < int.MaxValue))
                {
                    // Should not reach this point, but just in case we guard it with another condition
                    after = previous + 1;


                    arrList.Count = after;
                }
                else if (!(after == previous))
                {
                    // This should always be true, because Count property is a set only property.
                    bool isCountChanged = after != previous;


                    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$after", after, "After");


                    bool expected = isCountChanged;


                    bool actual = after == previous;


                    // ReSharper disable once ConvertSprintfToInterpolation
                    bool b1 = String.CompareOrdinal(String.Format("{0} : {1}", "Expected", expected), String.Format("{0} : {1}", "Actual", actual)) != 0;


                    if (b1)
                    {
                        bool b2 = expected;


                        // ReSharper disable once ConvertSprintfToInterpolation
                        Assert.AreEqual(String.Format("{0} : {1}", "Count property should always be set only property", b2), b2);
                    }


                    break;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}


A: The problem comes from your assumptions and preconditions. For instance, your assumption PexAssumeUnderTest indicates that the Count property should be greater than or equals to zero but it won't hold true in the actual application.

ArrayList does not allow setting the Count property directly. Instead, you should use Add or Remove methods to manipulate the elements instead of direct setting Count property.

PexHelper is not available in the current version of Pex so you might need to manually craft your PEX test cases.

So, your Pex test needs to include more scenarios which need to deal with Add and Remove methods instead of setting directly. Unfortunately, it's quite complicated and often need a lot of manual adjustment to get it work, since Pex can only generate some basic scenarios.

In addition, Pex might also have limitations based on its current implementation.

If you just want to test the Count property, just directly use Pex to test it like you did in your test: PUT_CountContract.

Hope this helps and feel free to reach out if you have any additional questions.

Update
Based on your more detailed requirements, it is best to generate a unit test case along these lines using Pex by focusing on the Add and Remove methods:

public void Pex_Test()
{
  ArrayList list = new ArrayList();
  int oldCount = 0;
  ArrayList listChanged = ArrayList.New(list);
  int newCount = 0;

  listChanged.Add("Element");
  newCount = listChanged.Count;
  Assert.IsTrue(newCount > oldCount); // should be increased by adding a new element

  listChanged.RemoveAt(0);
  newCount = listChanged.Count;
  Assert.IsTrue(newCount < oldCount); // should be reduced by removing an element

  // And you could write more test cases like above
}

Hope this helps and feel free to reach out if you have any additional questions.

Update 2
After reviewing your contract test, here is an updated code snippet for your contract test:

```
public partial class ArrayListContractTest
{
    [PexMethod]
    [PexExplore()]
    public void PEX_PUT_CountContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrayList)
    {
        int oldCount = arrayList.Count;
        arrayList.Add("Element");
        int newCount = arrayList.Count;

        Assert.IsTrue(newCount > oldCount); // should be increased by adding a new element

        arrayList.RemoveAt(0);
        newCount = arrayList.Count;
        Assert.IsTrue(newCount < oldCount); // should be reduced by removing--------------------
            Assume.True(arrList.Count>=0); //arrList.Count >= 0);
            


            int Old_Ret = arrList.Count;

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  for an arbitrary set of assumptions, @@@ pre-count value of array is less than or equal to after-count value of array

            Assume.True( Old_Ret <= arrList.Count);             //Old_Ret <= arrList.Count
        } 

I get error "PexInvariantException: The invariant 'Old_Ret <= arrList.Count' doesn't hold.". Why will Pex fail this test for Count property of ArrayList?

A: First, it's important to understand that Pex doesn't verify properties of objects, such as ArrayList's Count property. Instead, Pex attempts to explore different execution paths and verify edge cases. Thus, Pex doesn't verify whether the count will be increased, decreased or remains stable after you added an item to the list.

The problem in your post is that you used the post-condition to create an assumption (via the Assume.True() method). The problem with the code snippet is, that the post-condition you've provided can never hold true, because the number of elements in the Arraylist could never decrease (if you add an item, the count should increase).

Since this property (the count of the ArrayList increasing) is a functionality provided by arraylist, not a property that it has, Pex doesn't have the ability to verify it. When an ArrayList.Count increases, there is no explicit check that your code is doing, therefore Pex doesn't know to perform the "arrList.Count verification". 

The best you can do for this case is to ensure that the length of the array gets larger after you add items - while checking the pre and post condition on the ArrayList.Add operation.

Pex can ensure that a specific item gets added to the list when it's possible to add an item to the list:

[PexMethod]
public void CanAddItemToArrayList([PexAssumeValid]ArrayList arrList, [PexAssumeValid]object itemToAdd)
{
    int oldCount = arrList.Count;
    arrList.Add(itemToAdd);
    PexAssert.AreEqual(oldCount + 1, arrList.Count);
}

Above snippet checks if the added item did increase the arraylist's Count property.

If you have a complex scenario where counting has more rules (e.g., subtracts for Remove operations), you could program your own check rules that verify the list's count. However, this goes beyond the scope of Pex/DotNet/ML.

Remember, when Pex tests methods that manage collections, it only considers basic scenarios such as:

- empty collection
- single item added, multiple items removed

In more advanced scenarios, you have to provide your own verification logic.

For example:

You can assume that if your object is in a valid state, then your collection will be empty in a valid state:

[PexMethod]
public void CountContract([PexAssumeValid] TestObject target)
{
    Assume.True(target.SomeValidState);
    Assume.True(target.MyCollection.Count == 0);

    target.MyCollection.Add(new ArrayList());

    Assume.True(target.SomeValidState);
    Assume.True(target.MyCollection.Count >= 0);
    Assume.True(target.MyCollection.Count <= 1);
}

In the example above, the Pex test method asserts that after adding a new ArrayList to the collection, the count is not guaranteed to stay at 0, but can be 0 or 1.

In this example, Pex does not verify the property of the ArrayList, because the property is part of ArrayList.

In conclusion, with the current version of Pex we just rely on basic scenarios for collections and do not have the ability to define our own verification logic for these collections.

Hope this helps.

A: Pex only validates against the public APIs of the System.Collections.ArrayList class. If Pex determines that a call to ArrayList.Add() increases count by 1, it's the responsibility of the actual developer to verify the internal state.

It's easy to find a Pex property/method coverage that says you cover 100% (via PexMethod, PexConstructor, PexProperty etc.), but still, the internal state of a complex object might not be in sync with--------------------            Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0);
        }
    }
