```
## PexAssumeUnderTest 
This attribute specifies that a test method is a unit test for a method under test. The PexAssumeUnderTest attribute is used to mark the methods that correspond to the methods being tested in the class under test.

In the test method, you can use PexAssert.AreEqual or other Assert methods to specify conditions that are supposed to hold true about the output. You can also use PexObserve.Values to specify observed values.

When Pex runs the tests, it modifies the test method to generate a set of input values that satisfy the assertions, and outputs them.

## PexObserve.ValueForViewing (string, object)
This allows us to add a value that Pex will observe and display in the Pex Explorer, for verification purpose.

The first parameter is the name of the value, the second parameter is the actual value. For Pex to be able to display certain values that your method returns, return the value from this method and do return it.
  
## PexObserve.ValuesForViewing (string, object[])
This allows us to add values that you want to observe and display in Pex Explorer. 

The first parameter is the name of the values, the second parameter is the actual values. Returns the variable name that Pex will observe and display in the Pex Explorer.

## PexAssumeRefeences (string[], object[]) 
This attribute is used to explicitly state that some of the values that you specified may be affected by the method under test. By default, Pex assumes that all method inputs and outputs may be affected by them. This attribute allows you to specify which method inputs and outputs your method under test might affect. 

One input corresponds to one instance of the method under test. The elements of methodInputs specify which inputs will be modified by this instance of the method under test. 

## Further Information

Check out the Microsoft Research Pex documentation for more detail, including how to use the attribute with your specific test scenario: 
https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/quick-start-pex-for-testing-moles?view=vs-2019

And also this:
https://github.com/microsoft/ptvs/blob/master/Microsoft.Pex.Framework/doc/PexAllFiles.md

Note: The examples and detailed information shared in this response are for informational and learning purposes. The information and examples given here do not constitute financial, legal or other advice. It is extremely important to ensure compliance with all relevant local, state and federal laws when dealing with personal data and this advice is intended as such.

