A: The PexObserve.ValueForViewing is used for visual inspection of Pex generated tests. Its content will be shown when you run a Pex-engine session on a test with this method.
However, no assertion method is used within Pex-generated tests. They are there to guide the fuzzing process in a testing tool and may provide useful data for post-testing analysis.
For example, this line Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0); is not supposed to cause testing failures, it is merely used for visual representation of your assumptions.
The general rule is - use as many assertions as you would use in a non-Pex testing context, but remove the PexObserve methods. The Pex engine has its own way to verify and explore code branches. Thus, you should focus on the part of your code that is tested by unit tests (and not by Pex-generated tests).
Regarding the question "Can we use Debug.Assert in Pex generated tests?" - no, not directly. Debug.Assert is not run-time safe and you should not use it in tests. Thus it's better to use normal debug and unit test assertions.
Q: How to use Java to execute a command from a web application, like SSH commands I'm developing a web applications and I have some functionalities I want to support. One of the main features is to interact with a hardware device and for that I'm expecting to execute certain command through java. 
The commands I need to execute would be something like "reboot", "shutdown", "turn_on", etc.

Is there a library or any Java classes that I can use to interact with the hardware device? 

Currently, I am using a Shell Script for this and trying to execute this via java using ProcessBuilder or Runtime.getRuntime().exec(). However, I would like to use something more structured and easy to use.

In particular, I am looking for classes or methods in Java that allow me to interact with the command prompt interface (CLI), or execute any command on a remote system.

I know there are various libraries out there like JSch, Apache MINA SSHD, Jcraft, etc. But it would be great if they were able to help me in my requirements. 

My current need is essentially to execute commands which interact with hardware, like power on/off or reboot commands. I have a system where it's okay to have the command executed on the remote system. 

Any help would be much appreciated. If you have any suggestion about how to handle this kind of interactions, I would love to hear from you.


A: You can use Java's Runtime.exec(command) to execute commands on your local system (assuming the necessary software is installed and the system user has adequate permissions).
However, if you are interested in remotely executing commands on another system over a network (for example with SSH), you would need to use an SSH library. These libraries provide a secure channel over which commands can be sent and results can be captured. Two options I can think of are:


*

*JSch: A pure Java implementation of SSH2 and offers many functionalities. It's relatively easy to use and widely used. But like many other Java libraries, it may have a steeper learning curve.

*Apache MINA SSHD: A pure Java SSH Server, it may take some time to fully grasp the whole server-side model, but it can act as a server in conjunction with JSch and allow remote command execution.


For simpler use cases, Apache Commons Net library might be a good alternative. The package contains components to create, consume and process an internet standards like Email, FTP, and HTTP. It also includes SSH and Telnet package that makes it to handle most of the commonly used internet protocol, you might find them helpful.

A: Java might not be the best place to handle this sort of interaction (although it's certainly possible, and there are libraries available for this). Java is more typically used to interact with databases, web services, etc., although it's certainly capable of much more. For hardware interactions, it's usually best to use lower-level languages (like C or C++) and interact with them via Java through JNI or a library like JNA. 

If you want to stick with Java, consider using JSch as it's a robust and widely-used library for SSH interaction.  Here's an example of how you might use JSch to execute an SSH command:

import com.jcraft.jsch.JSch;
import com.jcraft.jsch.Session;--------------------